# signal: a POSIX program's signal channel (docs/M13-PLAN.md section 6,
# "Signals"). Whoever starts a POSIX program makes the channel and keeps
# its client end: posix_spawn (user/posix/sys/spawn.c), and the native
# shell for the POSIX programs it runs. The program gets the server end on
# its startup role SR_SIGNALS, and its signal thread
# (user/posix/sys/sigthread.c) reads it. Holding the client end is the
# power to signal the program: `kill(pid, sig)` of a child writes `send`
# on it (SIGKILL is process_kill on the process handle instead, which no
# program can ignore).
#
# Who serves it: the POSIX program's C library, never a service. Who
# calls it: the program's starter.
#
# Reconnect: the channel is made once per program and never made again.
# ERR_PEER_CLOSED on the client's side: the program has ended (a signal to
# a program that has ended is dropped, as Linux's kill of a zombie
# succeeds and does nothing). On the program's side: the starter has gone,
# and nothing will be sent again; the program goes on.

protocol signal 44

# How a signal was sent: by kill (or the shell's Ctrl+C, a terminal's
# hang-up), or by sigqueue with a value.
const CODE_USER  0
const CODE_QUEUE 1

# Signal `signo` (Linux's number, 1 to 64; never SIGKILL) to the program.
# `code` is CODE_USER or CODE_QUEUE; `value` is sigqueue's (0 for kill).
# One way: the sender learns nothing of what the program does with it. A
# full channel (ERR_SHOULD_WAIT) is the program falling behind: the
# sender never waits for room. A standard signal (1 to 31) it couldn't
# write is owed once, as Linux's pending set holds it, and written when
# the channel has room (a second one of that number meanwhile is the same
# one); a real-time signal is not owed: the sender's sigqueue is EAGAIN. A
# program drops what it can't use (a signal number out of range, SIGKILL,
# an unknown code) without a word.
1  send  (u8 signo, u8 code, i32 value) event

# The other way, the program's notices to its starter (docs/FORK-PLAN.md
# section 1.7): what a POSIX starter's table of children must hear of. A
# POSIX starter's signal thread reads them (user/posix/sys/proc.c); the
# native shell reads none (they wait in its end, a few bytes each, until
# the program has ended). The starter trusts nothing in them: one whose
# writer (CHANNEL_READ_PID) isn't the program, that breaks its format or
# names what the program can't be is dropped without a word, and one too
# big for any notice ends the starter's listening to that program. The
# program waits up to a second for room, then drops it.

# The program has exec'd: the new image is in place (or started, for one
# that can't be exec'd in place). Sent only by a vfork child, whose parent
# waits for it (or for the child's end) before vfork returns.
2  exec     -> () event
# The program's process group is now pgid (setpgid on itself): its own pid,
# or a group of its session.
3  group    -> (i32 pgid) event
# The program leads a session of its own now (setsid): its session and its
# process group are its pid.
4  session  -> () event

# The starter asks for the program's open descriptors (what /proc/<pid>/fd
# of a child lists, user/posix/sys/proc.c). `seq` comes back in the answer.
# A program with a /data program started for it (execve of one waits for
# it) passes the question on to that program and its answer back.
5  list_fds  (u32 seq) event
# The answer: the descriptors the program has open, as a bitmap: bit
# (n % 8) of byte (n / 8) is descriptor n; the bytes after the last one
# with a bit set are left out (none: no descriptor open). Sent once to a
# question; a starter that wants it waits for it a while and goes without.
6  fds       -> (u32 seq, u8[<=128] bits) event
